The Universe as a Space of Possibilities

Toward a First‑Principles Theory of Civilizational Discovery

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

We develop a mathematical foundation for the hypothesis that the fundamental activity of an intelligent civilization is to explore the space of physically possible configurations permitted by the laws of nature. Building on constructor theory, information theory, dynamical systems, and metric geometry, we formally define the possibility space \Omega, endow it with a metric structure, and model civilization as a controlled process that expands the realized subset R(t) \subseteq \Omega. We derive rigorous bounds on exploration speed from thermodynamics (Landauer, Bekenstein), computation (NP‑completeness, undecidability), and information theory (Kolmogorov complexity). Optimal exploration policies are studied via the Gittins index and Pontryagin’s maximum principle, constrained by the No Free Lunch theorem. Stability conditions draw on viability theory and early‑warning signals. Crucially, we propose concrete, falsifiable predictions linking the growth of R(t) to observable scaling laws in patent databases, scientific literature, and technological diversity. The result is a testable framework in which scientific and technological progress become measurable trajectories through an astronomically large, structured landscape of latent possibilities.

---

1. Introduction

For millennia, humanity has asked what exists, why the laws are as they are, what intelligence is, and why creativity emerges. This work treats these questions as facets of a single deeper principle: the universe is an immense repository of unrealized configurations, and intelligence is the physical process that progressively discovers and instantiates them.

We propose a first‑principles theory in which a civilization is not an accidental byproduct of evolution but a lawful process that expands the frontier between the actual and the possible. This paper provides the mathematical scaffolding for such a theory, translating the philosophical vision into rigorous, falsifiable statements. Unlike earlier purely descriptive accounts, we ground every claim in established physical, computational, and information‑theoretic bounds, and we specify exactly how the theory can be tested against empirical data.

---

2. Formal First Principles

We begin by restating the core intuitions as precise axioms.

Axiom 1 (Constructor‑theoretic lawfulness).
Physical laws are exactly the constraints on which transformations of a substrate are possible, and which are impossible, independent of the specific constructor (Deutsch & Marletto, 2014). Let \mathcal{T} be the set of all tasks x \rightarrow y where x, y are attributes of substrates. The laws of physics are the subset \mathcal{T}_{\text{poss}} \subseteq \mathcal{T} of tasks that can be carried out with arbitrarily high accuracy and repeatability. The possibility space \Omega is the set of all attributes x that can appear as a target of some possible task or as the initial state of some substrate. This formalizes Principle 1: physics fixes what is possible, not what is actual.

Axiom 2 (Immensity of the latent).
The laws of physics, while restrictive, still allow a combinatorially vast set of distinguishable configurations. In any finite region of space with finite energy, the number of possible quantum states is bounded by the Bekenstein bound |\Omega_{\text{local}}| \le \exp(2\pi R E / \hbar c \ln 2). For the observable universe, this number is \sim 10^{10^{122}}. The set \Omega is thus astronomically large but finite for any finite physical system. This encapsulates Principle 2.

Axiom 3 (Intelligence as discovery).
An intelligent agent (or civilization) does not create new physical laws; it discovers configurations x \in \Omega that were previously uninstantiated. Innovation is therefore a selection and realization of a point in \Omega that was not previously in the realized set R(t). This is Principle 3.

Axiom 4 (Expansion of the realized).
Civilizational activity increases the size and diversity of R(t) over time, unless constrained by resource depletion or collapse. The rate of growth of R(t)—specifically, the rate at which algorithmically novel configurations are added—is a measure of scientific and technological progress. This is Principle 4.

