5. The Civilizational Trajectory R(t): Adjacent Possible Dynamics

5.1 Realized set

R(t) \subseteq \Omega is the set of all macroscopic configurations that have been instantiated by the civilization up to time t. Instatiation means that a configuration has been physically realized at least once, whether as a tool, a scientific experiment, a cultural artifact, or a biological construct.

The process by which R(t) expands is captured by the adjacent possible framework. Kauffman’s concept—the set of possibilities that are one step away from what already exists—has been formalized as the Pólya Urn with Triggering (PUT) model (Loreto et al., 2016).

5.2 PUT expansion rule

We model R(t) as a multiset of “colors” (configurations). At each step:

· Draw a color uniformly.
· If it is a known color, add \rho copies of it to the urn (reinforcement).
· If it is a novel color (never drawn before), add \nu+1 brand‑new colors to the urn, plus \rho copies of the novel color.

This rule yields two empirically validated laws:

Theorem 5.1 (Heaps’ law). The number of distinct discoveries D(n) after n steps grows as a power law:

D(n) \propto n^\gamma, \quad 0 < \gamma < 1,

where \gamma depends on \rho and \nu.

Theorem 5.2 (Zipf’s law). The frequency of a discovered configuration of rank r scales as f(r) \propto r^{-\alpha} with \alpha \approx 1.

Proof sketch. The PUT process is a two‑parameter reinforcement‑plus‑innovation urn scheme. Its asymptotic behavior can be solved exactly via Yule‑process approximations or master equations, yielding the above scaling relations. These laws have been observed in the emergence of tags, Wikipedia edits, and other innovation datasets (Tria et al., 2014).

Thus, civilizational expansion is neither purely random nor deterministic but follows a universal scaling pattern governed by the combinatorial structure of \Omega.

5.3 Operationalizing R(t)

In practice, the realized set can be approximated by countable proxies: the set of all granted patents (each patent describing a distinct configuration), all published chemical compounds (molecular libraries), all distinct software packages, or all scientifically described species. While no proxy is perfect, these allow us to measure d|R|/dt and test Heaps’ law.

