6. Information‑Theoretic Measures of Innovation

6.1 Civilizational entropy

Let p_t(x) be the relative frequency (or fraction of total civilizational effort) devoted to configuration x \in R(t) at time t. The Shannon entropy

H(t) = -\sum_{x \in R(t)} p_t(x) \log p_t(x)

measures the diversity of the actively exploited configurations.

Note on monotonicity. Unlike an earlier claim, H(t) is not guaranteed to be non‑decreasing, because civilizations can forget, abandon configurations, or shift to monocultures. However, exploratory activity that adds genuinely new, frequently used configurations tends to increase H(t), while pure exploitation leaves it constant or may even decrease it if resources concentrate. We therefore define the innovation rate as the time derivative of H due specifically to the addition of novel, incompressible configurations:

\dot I(t) \equiv \left.\frac{dH}{dt}\right|_{\text{novel}}.

A civilization that purely exploits has \dot I = 0; one that actively explores has \dot I > 0.

6.2 Algorithmic novelty

The algorithmic information content of a new configuration x_{\text{new}} relative to the existing corpus R(t) is

\text{Nov}(x_{\text{new}}) = K(x_{\text{new}} \mid R(t)),

the length of the shortest program that outputs x_{\text{new}} given a full description of R(t). The invariance theorem guarantees this is well‑defined up to an additive constant independent of the universal machine.

Theorem 6.1 (Genuine novelty \Leftrightarrow incompressibility). If x_{\text{new}} is algorithmically derivable from existing knowledge, then K(x_{\text{new}} \mid R(t)) \approx 0. True breakthroughs have large conditional complexity. Moreover, Kolmogorov complexity is uncomputable—there is no algorithm to determine the novelty of an arbitrary string. This does not imply that intelligence requires non‑algorithmic processes, but it does show that no formal procedure can automate the recognition of all deep novelties; heuristic insight remains indispensable.

