7. Thermodynamic and Computational Bounds

7.1 Landauer’s principle

Recording a new, distinct macroscopic configuration necessitates irreversible state change at the micro level. Erasing one bit of information requires dissipating at least k_B T \ln 2 of heat (Landauer, 1961; experimentally confirmed by Bérut et al., 2012).

Proof sketch. Erasure maps two logical states to one, reducing physical entropy by \Delta S_{\text{sys}} = -k_B \ln 2. By the second law, the environment’s entropy must increase by at least that amount, giving Q \ge k_B T \ln 2.

7.2 Energy lower bound for civilizational memory

To maintain a memory of a realized set containing I bits of information (the minimal encoding of the distinction between realized and unrealized configurations), a civilization must spend at least

E_{\text{min}} = I \, k_B T \ln 2

of free energy. Note that I is not |\Omega| nor |R(t)| but the information content necessary to specify which configurations have been realized. If configurations are equiprobable and independent, I \approx |R(t)| \log_2(|\Omega|/|R(t)|); in practice, the encoding may be much more efficient. The expansion rate dI/dt is thus capped by the available free energy flux (e.g., stellar power).

7.3 Bekenstein bound and computational limits

The Bekenstein bound limits the total number of bits that can be stored in a region of radius R and energy E. Combining this with the light‑crossing time gives the maximum number of logical operations per second for a physical system, known as Lloyd’s limit (Lloyd, 2000): \sim 10^{51} ops/s for an Earth‑mass computer. This is far larger than any current engineering capability but represents an absolute physical ceiling.

No civilization can process information faster than this limit. Moreover, as argued below, the difficulty of searching \Omega can easily exceed any physically attainable computation rate.

7.4 Computational hardness of discovery

The landscape of possible configurations often has a rugged structure. In Kauffman’s NK model, a configuration is a binary string of length N, and fitness is an average of N local contributions each depending on K other bits (Kauffman & Levin, 1987). For K>0, the fitness landscape is highly correlated, and finding the globally fittest configuration is NP‑complete (Weinberger, 1991). This means that, in general, an optimal search through \Omega requires time exponential in N—a fundamental computational limit on civilizational exploration, unless heuristic shortcuts exist.

Furthermore, the reachability problem for analytic dynamical systems in \mathbb{R}^3 is Turing‑complete (Graça et al., 2008), proving that whether a desired configuration can be physically realized is undecidable in principle. However, the physical dynamics of our universe may not span all analytic vector fields; this result serves as a cautionary upper bound rather than a proven limitation on real civilizations.

