8. Optimal Exploration Policies

A civilization faces the explore–exploit dilemma: allocate resources to known, productive configurations or invest in probing unknown regions of \Omega. This maps directly to the multi‑armed bandit problem.

8.1 Gittins index policy

For independent exploration avenues (arms), each with unknown reward statistics, the Gittins index theorem (Gittins, 1979) provides an optimal policy: assign to each arm a dynamic index \nu_i depending only on its Bayesian posterior state, and always pursue the arm with the highest index. The index decomposes the high‑dimensional dynamic programming problem into independent one‑dimensional problems, enabling tractable optimal exploration.

8.2 Pontryagin’s maximum principle

If we model the exploration process as a controlled dynamical system \dot{x} = f(x,u), with state x representing the achieved configuration set and control u the research portfolio, then the optimal trajectory must satisfy Pontryagin’s principle (Pontryagin et al., 1962): there exists a co‑state (adjoint) p(t) such that the Hamiltonian H(x,u,p) = p \cdot f(x,u) + \text{reward rate} is maximized by u^*(t) at every instant. This gives necessary conditions for the optimal allocation of civilizational resources to expand R(t).

8.3 The No Free Lunch constraint

The No Free Lunch (NFL) theorem (Wolpert & Macready, 1997) states that, averaged over all possible fitness landscapes, any two search algorithms are equivalent. Thus, an “optimal exploration policy” can exist only because \Omega has structure—correlations, local smoothness, adjacent‑possible connectivity—that makes some landscapes more plausible than others. The adjacent possible and Fisher information geometry are therefore not just descriptive but essential: they provide the bias that lifts the NFL degeneracy, making efficient discovery possible.

