9. Geometric and Topological Signatures of Discovery

9.1 Information geometry as the manifold of possibilities

When configurations form a parametric family, the Fisher metric (Section 4.1) turns that subset of \Omega into a Riemannian manifold \mathcal{M}. The civilizational trajectory \gamma(t) \in \mathcal{M} can be described by a stochastic differential equation:

d\gamma = \mu(\gamma,t) dt + \sigma(\gamma,t) dW_t,

where \mu represents theory‑driven directed search and \sigma dW_t captures serendipitous discovery. The volume of the reachable set grows with time, and novel discoveries correspond to exiting the current geodesic ball.

9.2 Persistent homology

Even when \Omega lacks an a priori metric, we can study the shape of R(t) as a point cloud once a meaningful embedding (e.g., via word embeddings of patents or molecular fingerprints) is chosen. Construct the Vietoris–Rips complex for a range of scales \epsilon. The persistent homology tracks birth and death of topological features (connected components, loops, voids). A new feature with long persistence signals the emergence of a qualitatively new structure. The stability theorem (Cohen-Steiner et al., 2007) guarantees that small perturbations in the data produce small changes in the persistence diagram, making the method robust.

Thus, the appearance of a new long‑persistence homology class in R(t) is a mathematically provable, noise‑resilient signature of a conceptual breakthrough.

