10. Stability and Viability Conditions

A civilization must maintain its own existence (stay within a safe operating space K) while pushing the frontier R(t).

10.1 Viability theory

For a dynamical system \dot{x} \in F(x) with state x (including resource levels, environmental variables), a closed set K is viable if there exists at least one trajectory staying in K forever. The Nagumo–Bouligand theorem (Aubin, 1991) gives a necessary and sufficient condition:

\forall x \in \partial K, \quad F(x) \cap T_K(x) \neq \emptyset,

where T_K(x) is the contingent cone. For civilization, this translates to: at every point on the boundary of viability (e.g., critical resource depletion), there must be an available control (innovation, conservation) that keeps the system inside K.

10.2 Early‑warning signals

Empirically, many complex systems exhibit critical slowing down before a collapse: rising variance and lag‑1 autocorrelation (Scheffer et al., 2009). A formal measure for civilizational risk can be constructed by tracking these indicators in resource‑flow networks or energetic‑informational balance. Several dynamical‑systems models of civilizational collapse (e.g., the HANDY model by Motesharrei et al., 2014) show that collapse risk rises when the rate of complexity increase outruns the rate of energy availability, an explicit mathematical formulation of Tainter’s diminishing returns on complexity.

