11. The Civilizational Hypothesis as a Formal Statement

We are now in a position to state the central hypothesis of this theory with mathematical precision.

Hypothesis (Civilizational Exploration Principle).
Let \Omega be a finite possibility space structured by constructor‑theoretic laws, endowed with appropriate metrics. A civilization at time t is characterized by a realized set R(t) \subseteq \Omega and a probability distribution p_t over R(t) representing the relative usage of each configuration. The civilizational objective is not merely survival but the sustainable maximization of the innovation rate \dot I(t) = dH(p_t)/dt, subject to:

1. Thermodynamic bound: The energy cost of maintaining and expanding memory is at least k_B T \ln 2 per bit of stored information (Landauer).
2. Informational bound: Total stored information cannot exceed the Bekenstein bound, I_{\text{stored}} \le S_{\text{Bekenstein}} / k_B \ln 2.
3. Computational bound: Searching \Omega in general landscapes may require time exponential in the number of interacting variables; the physical realizability of an arbitrary target is undecidable.
4. Stability constraint: The state of the civilization must remain within a viability kernel K, satisfying the Nagumo condition.
5. Structural advantage: The adjacent possible correlations and local smoothness (Fisher metric) reduce the effective search space, partially evading the No Free Lunch theorem and enabling polynomial‑time discovery under plausible physical priors.

This formulation transforms the philosophical vision into a testable, scientific program.

