Appendix A. Formal Definition of the Possibility Space \Omega

A.1 Constructor‑theoretic foundation

We adopt the framework of constructor theory (Deutsch & Marletto, 2014). A substrate is any physical system capable of possessing attributes (e.g., a memory cell with states 0 or 1, a gas with pressure p, a DNA sequence). A task is a transformation \mathbf{T} = \{x \to y\} on the set of attributes of a substrate. A task is possible (denoted x \to y) if there exists a constructor — a physical system that can cause the substrate to undergo the transformation with arbitrarily high accuracy and repeatability, while remaining unchanged in its ability to do so. Otherwise the task is impossible.

Let \mathcal{T} be the set of all tasks x \to y. The laws of physics are the subset \mathcal{T}_{\text{poss}} \subseteq \mathcal{T} of all possible tasks. The possibility space \Omega is the set of all attributes that can appear either as an input or as an output of some possible task:

\Omega = \{ x \mid \exists y \text{ such that } (x \to y) \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{poss}} \text{ or } (y \to x) \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{poss}} \}.

Note that \Omega is not necessarily a set of microscopic states; it is a set of operationally distinguishable attribute‑states, coarse‑grained by the available constructor‑based distinguishability. Two microstates correspond to the same attribute if no possible task can distinguish them (i.e., they behave identically under all possible measurements realizable by constructors).

A.2 Preorder and monoid structure

Define a relation \preceq on \Omega by

x \preceq y \quad \iff \quad (x \to y) \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{poss}}.

Because identity tasks x \to x are trivially possible (a constructor that does nothing), \preceq is reflexive. If x \preceq y and y \preceq z, then the composition (x \to z) is also possible (a constructor for x \to y followed by one for y \to z yields a constructor for x \to z), so \preceq is transitive. Hence (\Omega, \preceq) is a preorder.

Furthermore, consider the set of equivalence classes under mutual possibility: x \sim y iff x \preceq y and y \preceq x. The quotient \Omega/\!\sim forms a partially ordered set. The composition of tasks gives rise to an associative binary operation on equivalence classes, making it an ordered commutative monoid (Marletto, 2021). This algebraic structure formalises the notion of which configurations can be reached from which, and is foundational for understanding directed exploration.

A.3 Macroscopic versus microscopic \Omega

A crucial subtlety: the Bekenstein bound limits the number of orthogonal quantum states (microstates) in a region. A macroscopic configuration “chair” or “computer” is an equivalence class of microstates that are indistinguishable under the coarse‑graining defined by the available constructors. The number of such macroscopic attributes is bounded by the number of distinct partitions of the microstate set, but it is still finite and astronomically large. For our purposes, it suffices that |\Omega| is finite and that the bound |\Omega| \le \exp(S_{\text{Bekenstein}}/k_B \ln 2) holds for any physically admissible coarse‑graining, because each macroscopic attribute must correspond to a non‑empty subset of microstates, and the total number of microstates limits the total number of such subsets.

