Appendix B. The Bekenstein Bound and the Immensity of \Omega

B.1 Statement of the bound

Theorem B.1 (Bekenstein, 1981). For any physical system of energy E and enclosed within a sphere of radius R in asymptotically flat spacetime, the entropy S satisfies

S \le \frac{2\pi k_B}{\hbar c} R E.

Equivalently, the number of accessible orthogonal quantum states W is bounded by

W \le \exp\!\left( \frac{2\pi R E}{\hbar c \ln 2} \right).

This bound follows from the generalized second law of thermodynamics and black hole physics. A heuristic derivation: if a system violated the bound, one could drop it into a black hole and decrease total entropy, violating the generalized second law. Rigorous proofs exist within quantum field theory (Casini, 2008).

B.2 Application to \Omega

Take the observable universe: R \sim 10^{26} m, E \sim 10^{70} J. Then

|\Omega| \le \exp\!\left( \frac{2\pi \times 10^{26} \times 10^{70}}{1.05\times10^{-34} \times 3\times10^8 \times 0.693} \right) \sim 10^{10^{122}}.

Thus \Omega is finite and extraordinarily vast. Any realized set R(t) is a subset, so |R(t)| \le |\Omega|. The immensity ensures that at any practical epoch, |R(t)| \ll |\Omega| — the frontier is always open.

