Appendix C. Metric Structures on \Omega

C.1 Fisher information metric (parametric families)

Consider a family of configurations smoothly parameterised by \theta = (\theta^1,\dots,\theta^n) \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n. Suppose each parameter \theta defines a probability distribution p(x|\theta) over measurement outcomes x. The Fisher information matrix is

g_{ij}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \frac{\partial \log p(x|\theta)}{\partial\theta^i} \frac{\partial \log p(x|\theta)}{\partial\theta^j} \right].

This is a Riemannian metric on \Theta, unique up to reparameterisation as the only metric invariant under sufficient statistics (Amari, 2016). The geodesic distance

d_F(\theta_1,\theta_2) = \min_{\gamma} \int_0^1 \sqrt{\dot\gamma^i \dot\gamma^j g_{ij}(\gamma(t))}\, dt

quantifies the minimal statistical distinguishability between \theta_1 and \theta_2. In the context of \Omega, a subset that forms a design space (e.g., all possible proteins with a given fold) can be endowed with this metric.

C.2 Algorithmic information distance

We fix a universal prefix Turing machine U. The Kolmogorov complexity K(x) of a binary string x is the length of the shortest program that prints x and halts. The conditional complexity K(x|y) is the length of the shortest program that prints x given y as an input.

Definition C.1. The normalized information distance is

d_K(x,y) = \frac{\max\{K(x|y), K(y|x)\}}{\max\{K(x), K(y)\}}.

Theorem C.2 (Li & Vitányi, 2008). d_K is a metric on the set of all binary strings up to an additive constant, i.e., for all x,y,z,

· d_K(x,y) \ge 0, with equality iff x = y up to an additive constant,
· d_K(x,y) = d_K(y,x),
· d_K(x,z) \le d_K(x,y) + d_K(y,z) + O(1/\max(K(x),K(y),K(z))).

Proof sketch. Symmetry is obvious. The triangle inequality follows from the chain rule for Kolmogorov complexity K(x|z) \le K(x|y) + K(y|z) + O(1). The normalization ensures the constant‑additive caveat does not overwhelm the metric property.

The invariance theorem guarantees that for any other universal machine U', |K_U(x) - K_{U'}(x)| \le c for all x, so the metric structure is machine‑independent up to a global additive constant. Thus d_K captures an intrinsic “conceptual distance” independent of encoding details.

C.3 Thermodynamic distance

Define a path x \to y as a sequence of intermediate macrostates achievable by constructors, each transition requiring some minimum free energy input \Delta F_i. Let

d_{th}(x,y) = \inf_{\text{paths } x \to y} \sum_i \Delta F_i.

By Landauer’s principle, the free energy cost of altering information is k_B T \ln 2 per bit of irreversibly processed information. Therefore d_{th} provides a lower bound on the physical resources needed to move from one configuration to another.

