Appendix E. Thermodynamic Bounds on Exploration

E.1 Landauer’s principle

Theorem E.1 (Landauer, 1961). In any logically irreversible operation, such as erasing one bit of information, at least k_B T \ln 2 of heat is dissipated into the environment at temperature T.

Proof. Consider a memory bit that stores one of two equiprobable logical states. Its physical entropy (Boltzmann) is S_\text{init} = k_B \ln 2. Erasing the bit maps both logical states to a single “reset” state, reducing the number of accessible microstates; the final entropy is S_\text{final} = 0. Hence the system’s entropy decreases by \Delta S_\text{sys} = -k_B \ln 2. The environment receives heat Q and its entropy increases by at least \Delta S_\text{env} = Q/T. By the second law, \Delta S_\text{total} = \Delta S_\text{sys} + \Delta S_\text{env} \ge 0, so Q \ge k_B T \ln 2. The bound is achievable with quasi‑static erasure.

E.2 Cost of storing a realized set

To store the identity of a set R(t) requires a memory that distinguishes between |R(t)| realized items and the rest of \Omega. The minimal amount of information that must be stored is the self‑information of the set: I = \log_2 \binom{|\Omega|}{|R(t)|} bits if configurations are equiprobable a priori. In any efficient coding, the memory size scales as I, not as |R(t)| directly. Erasing or updating this memory as R(t) expands has an associated free‑energy cost.

Hence the energy flux \Phi available to the civilization imposes a bound on the rate of information change:

\frac{dI}{dt} \le \frac{\Phi}{k_B T \ln 2}.

Since the innovation rate \dot I(t) is tied to changes in R(t) and the entropy of distribution p_t, this energy‑information link provides a testable coupling.

E.3 Lloyd’s ultimate computational limit

The Bekenstein bound combined with the Margolus–Levitin theorem gives the maximum number of logical operations per second for a system of energy E and linear size R:

\dot N_{\text{ops}} \lesssim \frac{E R}{\hbar c}.

For an Earth‑mass system, this is roughly 10^{51} ops/s. This far exceeds current computational capabilities but is the absolute speed limit for any physical computation, and thus bounds the rate at which a civilization can explore \Omega algorithmically.

