Appendix F. Computational Hardness of Discovery

F.1 NP‑completeness of the NK landscape

The NK fitness landscape is defined by a binary string \mathbf{g} = (g_1,\dots,g_N) and a fitness function f(\mathbf{g}) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N f_i(g_i, g_{j_1},\dots,g_{j_K}), where each f_i depends on K other bits chosen randomly. For K>0, the landscape is highly correlated but rugged.

Theorem F.1 (Weinberger, 1991). For K \ge 2, the problem of finding the global maximum of the NK fitness function is NP‑complete.

Proof sketch. The reduction is from the NP‑complete problem 3‑SAT. Each clause is encoded into a local fitness contribution such that the overall fitness equals 1 if and only if the 3‑SAT formula is satisfiable. Because any deterministic algorithm can solve 3‑SAT in polynomial time only if P=NP, the optimization problem on generic NK landscapes is computationally intractable in the worst case.

Thus, unless P=NP, no general efficient algorithm exists for exploring \Omega when it resembles a rugged NK landscape.

F.2 Undecidability of reachability

Theorem F.2 (Graça et al., 2008). The question of whether a polynomial ordinary differential equation (ODE) in \mathbb{R}^3 reaches a given target region from a given initial condition is Turing‑complete.

This implies that the physical realizability of certain configurations may be undecidable: there exists no algorithmic procedure that can determine, in advance, whether a desired configuration can be created by a continuous physical process described by certain differential equations. Since our physical laws are expected to be expressible as differential equations, this places a fundamental limitation on predictive knowledge of the frontier of \Omega.

