Appendix G. Optimal Exploration Policies

G.1 Gittins index for multi‑armed bandits

Consider K independent research directions (“arms”). When arm i is chosen at time t, it yields a random reward R_{i,t} drawn from an unknown distribution with prior \pi_i. The objective is to maximize the expected discounted sum of future rewards \mathbb{E}[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \beta^t R_{a_t,t}], where \beta \in (0,1) is a discount factor and a_t the chosen arm.

Theorem G.1 (Gittins, 1979). For each arm i there exists a real‑valued index \nu_i(\pi_i), depending only on the arm’s current Bayesian posterior, such that the optimal policy is to always play the arm with the largest index.

The index is defined as

\nu_i(\pi_i) = \sup_{\tau > 0} \frac{\mathbb{E}\!\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\tau-1} \beta^t R_{i,t} \mid \pi_i \right]}{\mathbb{E}\!\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\tau-1} \beta^t \mid \pi_i \right]},

where \tau is a stopping time. This decomposes the multi‑dimensional dynamic programming problem into K independent one‑dimensional optimization problems, yielding a tractable optimal strategy.

In the civilizational context, “arms” correspond to distinct fields of exploration (e.g., particle physics, biotechnology). The Gittins index theorem formalizes the optimal allocation of resources to maximize the discounted sum of innovations.

G.2 Pontryagin’s maximum principle

Consider a continuous‑time controlled dynamical system:

\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t), u(t)), \qquad x(0) = x_0,

with state x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n representing the achieved configuration set and controls u(t) \in U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m representing the research portfolio. We wish to maximize a cumulative reward functional

J = \int_0^T L(x(t), u(t))\, dt + \phi(x(T)).

Theorem G.2 (Pontryagin et al., 1962). If u^*(t) is an optimal control and x^*(t) the corresponding optimal state trajectory, then there exists a costate (adjoint) vector p(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n such that

1. State equation: \dot{x}^* = \nabla_p H(x^*, u^*, p),
2. Costate equation: \dot{p} = -\nabla_x H(x^*, u^*, p),
3. Maximization: H(x^*(t), u^*(t), p(t)) = \max_{u \in U} H(x^*(t), u, p(t)) for almost every t \in [0,T],
4. Transversality: p(T) = \nabla_x \phi(x^*(T)),

where the Hamiltonian is defined as H(x,u,p) = p^T f(x,u) + L(x,u).

This necessary condition provides a powerful framework for analysing the optimal allocation of civilizational resources over time, once a concrete model for f and L is given.

