Appendix H. No Free Lunch Theorem and Structural Bias

Theorem H.1 (Wolpert & Macready, 1997). For any pair of deterministic search algorithms a_1 and a_2, the average performance over all possible discrete cost functions f: X \to Y is exactly identical:

\sum_f P(d_m^y \mid f, m, a_1) = \sum_f P(d_m^y \mid f, m, a_2),

where d_m^y is the sequence of m evaluated points and their costs.

Proof sketch. The theorem follows from the fact that without any restriction on f, the next unevaluated point is equally likely to have any cost regardless of past evaluations. Therefore, no algorithm can consistently outperform random search when averaged over all possible landscapes.

The No Free Lunch theorem implies that an “optimal exploration policy” can exist only because \Omega is not an arbitrary set: it possesses structure. The adjacent‑possible correlations (PUT) and local smoothness (Fisher metric) are precisely the biases that make directed exploration more efficient than random search. This justifies the central role of such structural properties in our framework.

