Appendix I. Viability Theory and Early Warning Signals

I.1 Nagumo condition for viability

Let X = \mathbb{R}^n and consider a differential inclusion \dot{x} \in F(x) with set‑valued map F. A closed set K \subset X is viable under F if for every initial state x_0 \in K, there exists at least one solution x(t) starting at x_0 that remains in K for all t \ge 0.

Theorem I.1 (Nagumo, 1942; Aubin, 1991). K is viable if and only if

\forall x \in \partial K, \quad F(x) \cap T_K(x) \neq \emptyset,

where T_K(x) is the contingent cone (or Bouligand tangent cone) defined by

T_K(x) = \left\{ v \in X \mid \liminf_{h \to 0^+} \frac{d(x+hv, K)}{h} = 0 \right\}.

This condition means that at every boundary point, there is an available velocity that points inside or tangentially to the viable set. For a civilization, K represents the region of resource, environmental, and social parameters that guarantee continued existence. The Nagumo condition gives a precise mathematical test for sustainable trajectories.

I.2 Early‑warning signals: critical slowing down

Near a fold bifurcation of a dynamical system, the dominant eigenvalue of the linearization approaches zero, causing the system’s recovery rate from small perturbations to decrease. This critical slowing down leads to:

· Rising variance: \text{Var}(x) \propto 1/\lambda for white noise perturbations (Carpenter & Brock, 2006),
· Rising autocorrelation: the lag‑1 autocorrelation \rho_1 \to 1 as \lambda \to 0.

These are generic, model‑free signals of an approaching tipping point (Scheffer et al., 2009). Within our framework, a civilization’s state x(t) could be monitored through proxies such as energy consumption per capita, information entropy, or resource depletion rates; a simultaneous increase in variance and autocorrelation would signal imminent loss of viability.

