Appendix J. Operationalizing Predictions

We outline a concrete empirical programme to test the theory.

J.1 Data sources

· Patents: US Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO) database, European Patent Office (EPO). Each patent represents a claimed new configuration. The set of all granted patents (or a time‑series of applications) serves as a proxy for R(t).
· Scientific publications: arXiv, PubMed. The appearance of new concepts or entities (genes, materials, programming languages) can be tracked.
· Molecular libraries: Chemical databases such as PubChem or ZINC. Each new molecule represents a point in a chemical possibility space.
· Energy data: World Bank, IEA statistics on total primary energy supply.

J.2 Testing Heaps’ and Zipf’s laws

For patents, we let n be the cumulative number of patent applications and D(n) the number of distinct 4‑digit IPC (International Patent Classification) subclasses used. The hypothesis \gamma < 1 vs \gamma = 1 (constant novelty rate) can be tested by regressing \log D against \log n. Zipf’s law can be tested on the frequency of IPC codes or citation counts.

J.3 Energy–entropy coupling

Compute a time series of civilizational entropy H(t) from the distribution of patents across technology classes (or scientific publications across fields). The innovation rate \dot I(t) is the derivative of H(t) (smoothed). Compare this with the growth rate of total energy consumption per capita. Under the theory, periods of rising \dot I should correlate with rising per‑capita energy availability; stagnation or decline in \dot I should follow energy plateaus. Granger‑causality tests can assess the predictive relationship.

J.4 Topological breakpoints

Embed patents as vectors using text‑mining (e.g., TF‑IDF of claims or titles) and construct a point cloud for each year. Compute persistent homology and detect long‑lived topological features. Dates when new long‑persistence homology classes appear can be compared with expert‑identified paradigm shifts (e.g., invention of the transistor, discovery of CRISPR). A statistically significant correspondence would support the geometric model.

